\section{Results}
\subsection{Main comparison}
Table~\ref{tab:main} and Fig.~\ref{fig:difficulty} give success against difficulty. At difficulty 3 every agent that chooses among testable macro-actions solves almost all episodes (Hypothesis @{SR('hypothesis',3)}, PSRL @{SR('psrl',3)}, Macro-FF @{SR('macro_ff',3)}, Macro-GRU @{SR('macro_gru',3)}), against @{SR('reactive',3)} for the reactive agent. Primitive-action PPO reaches @{SR('ppo_gru',3)}; it learned almost nothing in 1.2M steps on one core, so it is an under-trained baseline and says nothing about deep RL in general. The hypothesis agent beats the reactive agent at difficulty 3, 4 and 5 (@{PS('d=3: Hypothesis vs Reactive')}; @{PS('d=4: Hypothesis vs Reactive')}; @{PS('d=5: Hypothesis vs Reactive')}).

The more informative comparisons are with the other macro-level agents. Against PSRL the hypothesis agent is @{WORD('d=3: Hypothesis vs PSRL')} at difficulty 3 and @{WORD('d=4: Hypothesis vs PSRL')} at 4 and 5 (@{PS('d=4: Hypothesis vs PSRL')}; @{PS('d=5: Hypothesis vs PSRL')}). Against the learned Macro-GRU, which has the same actions but no belief features, it is @{WORD('d=3: Hypothesis vs Macro-GRU')} at difficulty 3 and clearly higher at 4 and 5 (@{PS('d=4: Hypothesis vs Macro-GRU')}; @{PS('d=5: Hypothesis vs Macro-GRU')}); Macro-FF gives the same picture (@{PS('d=5: Hypothesis vs Macro-FF')} at difficulty 5). The learned agents' intervals reflect @{NMODELS} independently trained models; the spread between models is not small at the hard levels, with Macro-GRU at difficulty 5 ranging over @{spread[(spread.agent=='macro_gru')&(spread.setting=='d=5')]['min'].iloc[0]!.2f}--@{spread[(spread.agent=='macro_gru')&(spread.setting=='d=5')]['max'].iloc[0]!.2f}.

@@TABLE_MAIN@@

\begin{figure}[!htbp]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=0.78\textwidth]{figures/fig_difficulty.pdf}
\caption{Success against difficulty. Markers are means; bars are 95\% bootstrap intervals over units (trained models for learned agents, evaluation seeds otherwise). The Hypothesis and Hypothesis-adaptive curves nearly coincide.}\label{fig:difficulty}
\end{figure}

\subsection{A matched-information control}
The comparison above gives the hypothesis agent something the learned macro agents do not have: a running belief over bindings and the value and information gain computed from it. To separate that from the act of explicit testing, we trained Macro-GRU+belief, which sees the same two quantities for each candidate; nothing else about it changes. With @{NM('macro_gruB')} training runs it reaches @{SR('macro_gruB',4)} at difficulty 4 and @{SR('macro_gruB',5)} at difficulty 5, against @{SR('macro_gru',4)} and @{SR('macro_gru',5)} without the beliefs (@{PS('d=4: Macro-GRU+belief vs Macro-GRU')}; @{PS('d=5: Macro-GRU+belief vs Macro-GRU')}). Against the hypothesis agent, the data cannot separate it at difficulty 4 (@{PS('d=4: Hypothesis vs Macro-GRU+belief')}) or at difficulty 5 (@{PS('d=5: Hypothesis vs Macro-GRU+belief')}). With @{NM('macro_gruB')} runs the intervals are still wide, so failing to separate the two agents means the experiment did not detect a difference of the size seen for the other learned agents; it does not show that the true performances are equal. The reading we draw is that most of the learned agents' deficit comes from missing belief information, not from learning being unable to act on it.

\textbf{Supplied versus learned.} What the tracker supplies to Macro-GRU+belief is, for each candidate macro, the expected value and the information gain computed from the hypothesis confidences; producing those numbers already needs the rule templates, the episodic memory and the exclusivity rule. What the network still has to learn is how to turn them into choices: which macro to take, how to trade information against path cost, how to cope with distractors. What is \emph{not} tested is whether a network could derive such beliefs from raw observations. The result therefore says that the learned agents' deficit is largely a deficit of belief information. It does not show that symbolic reasoning beats deep learning. With @{NM('macro_gruB')} runs of the belief agent, the smallest difference detectable with 80\% power at difficulty 5 is about @{MDE('macro_gruB',5)!.2f}.

@@TABLE_P1@@

\subsection{Generalization to unseen rules}
Table~\ref{tab:gen} and Fig.~\ref{fig:regimes} give held-out success. On recombination and composition (A, B, E) all macro-level agents stay near the top, and on size alone (C2) larger grids are not harder, so the negative gaps there reflect easier test grids. Longer chains (C) cost the learned agents about @{GAP('macro_gru','C_chain_length')!.2f} but not the hypothesis agent (@{GAP('hypothesis','C_chain_length')!.2f}). Only regime D uses @{NM('macro_gru','D_rule_permutation')} trained models; regimes A, B, C, C2 and E use 3 per learned agent, so their intervals are indicative only.

The decisive contrast is regime D, where the test colors are disjoint from the training colors and the relation is unchanged. Macro-GRU scores @{TE('macro_gru','D_rule_permutation','train_heldout')!.3f} on held-out training-distribution environments and @{TE('macro_gru','D_rule_permutation')!.3f} on unseen colors, and Macro-FF @{TE('macro_ff','D_rule_permutation')!.3f}, while the hypothesis agent is unaffected (train @{TE('hypothesis','D_rule_permutation','train_heldout')!.3f}, test @{TE('hypothesis','D_rule_permutation')!.3f}). The gaps over @{NM('macro_gru','D_rule_permutation')} trained models are @{GAP('macro_gru','D_rule_permutation')!.2f} (GRU) and @{GAP('macro_ff','D_rule_permutation')!.2f} (FF), and the hypothesis agent beats both (@{PS('D: Hypothesis vs Macro-GRU')}; @{PS('D: Hypothesis vs Macro-FF')}). The belief-augmented agent scores @{TE('macro_gruB','D_rule_permutation')!.3f}, indistinguishable from the hypothesis agent (@{PS('D: Hypothesis vs Macro-GRU+belief')}). Its features do not mention colors, which fits the failure being one of symbol-specific policies. PSRL is lower than the hypothesis agent in this regime (@{PS('D: Hypothesis vs PSRL')}).

