\subsection{What matters inside the agent}
The hypothesis and PSRL agents differ in two ways, expected-value versus sampled reward and the presence of an information bonus, so we crossed them (Fig.~\ref{fig:info}c; difficulty 5, differences with unadjusted 95\% intervals over evaluation seeds). Raising $\lambda$ from 0 to 8 changed success from @{FX('hyp_EV_l0',5)} to @{FX('hyp_EV_l8',5)} with expected-value reward (@{DIFF(fx[fx.difficulty==5],'hyp_EV_l8','hyp_EV_l0')}) and from @{FX('psrl_S_l0',5)} to @{FX('psrl_S_l8',5)} with sampled reward (@{DIFF(fx[fx.difficulty==5],'psrl_S_l8','psrl_S_l0')}). With $\lambda=0$ the reward form made no detectable difference (@{DIFF(fx[fx.difficulty==5],'hyp_EV_l0','psrl_S_l0')}); with $\lambda=8$ expected-value reward was ahead (@{DIFF(fx[fx.difficulty==5],'hyp_EV_l8','psrl_S_l8')}). At difficulty 4, memory and revision are necessary: success is @{A('full_memory')} with full memory and @{A('no_memory')} with none, and @{A('revision_on')} with revision against @{A('revision_off')} without. Choosing macros by expected value beats choosing them at random (@{A('info_driven')} against @{A('random_explore')}). The action cost matters too: $\mu=0.2$ drops success to @{A('mu_0.2')}. The information bonus helps most where distractors make uncertainty costly (Fig.~\ref{fig:info}a): at difficulty 5, success rises from @{A('lambda_0.0_d5')} at $\lambda=0$ to @{A('lambda_8.0_d5')} at $\lambda=8$.

\begin{figure}[!htbp]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=0.7\textwidth]{figures/fig_infobonus.pdf}
\caption{(a) Information weight $\lambda$ at difficulties 4 and 5. (b) Action-cost weight $\mu$ at difficulty 4. (c) Reward form crossed with the information bonus. Intervals are 95\% bootstrap intervals over evaluation seeds.}\label{fig:info}
\end{figure}

\subsection{When the structural assumption is false}
The hypothesis agent assumes that a key opens at most one door. When one key opens several doors it succeeds in @{MK('hypothesis','master_key_d3')!.3f} (difficulty 3) and @{MK('hypothesis','master_key_d4')!.3f} (difficulty 4) of episodes, against @{MK('hyp_nonexcl','master_key_d3')!.3f} and @{MK('hyp_nonexcl','master_key_d4')!.3f} without the assumption; dropping it costs @{1-MK('hyp_nonexcl','exclusive_control_d4')!.3f} of episodes when the assumption is true (difficulty 4, success @{MK('hyp_nonexcl','exclusive_control_d4')!.3f} against @{MK('hypothesis','exclusive_control_d4')!.3f}). PSRL, which resamples when its model offers no progress, is robust (@{MK('psrl','master_key_d3')!.3f}, @{MK('psrl','master_key_d4')!.3f}). The failure limits any claim that explicit testing is inherently more robust: it is only as robust as the assumptions built into its hypothesis space.

The adaptive agent turns the hard rule into a prior that is dropped on detectable violations (Sect.~\ref{sec:agents}). On the master-key environments it succeeds in @{MK('hyp_adaptive','master_key_d3')!.3f} and @{MK('hyp_adaptive','master_key_d4')!.3f} of episodes (@{PS('master_key_d3: Hyp.-adaptive vs Hypothesis')}; @{PS('master_key_d4: Hyp.-adaptive vs Hypothesis')}). On the exclusive control it is indistinguishable from the hard-assumption agent at difficulty 4 (@{PS('exclusive_control_d4: Hyp.-adaptive vs Hypothesis')}) and clearly ahead of the agent that never assumes exclusivity (@{PS('exclusive_control_d4: Hyp.-adaptive vs Hyp.-nonexcl.')}). It also matches the original agent on the standard difficulties (difference at difficulty 5: @{PS('d=5: Hypothesis vs Hyp.-adaptive')}). So in this setting the price of the assumption can be removed without giving up its benefit. This is evidence for one detectable kind of violation, not for robustness to structural misspecification in general.

@@TABLE_P3@@

\begin{figure}[!htbp]
