\includegraphics[width=0.7\textwidth]{figures/fig_assumption.pdf}
\caption{Master-key environments (one key opens several doors) and exclusive controls. The hard exclusivity assumption collapses; the adaptive version does not.}\label{fig:assumption}
\end{figure}

\subsection{Noisy feedback}
The deterministic environments make a failed test conclusive. To see what happens when it is not, a correct key fails to open its door with probability $\epsilon$ (a jammed lock, with the same feedback as a wrong key), for $\epsilon\in\{0,0.2,0.4\}$ at difficulties 3 and 4 (30 seeds of 20 environments). Learned agents were trained without noise, so for them this is a test-time shift and not noise-aware training. We also include a hypothesis agent that needs three contradictions instead of two before rejecting a hypothesis (Hypothesis-robust). At $\epsilon=0.4$ and difficulty 3 the hypothesis agent keeps @{SRN('hypothesis',3,0.4)} success against @{SRN('psrl',3,0.4)} for PSRL (@{PS('slip 0.4, d=3: Hypothesis vs PSRL')}), and at difficulty 4 @{SRN('hypothesis',4,0.4)} against @{SRN('psrl',4,0.4)} (@{PS('slip 0.4, d=4: Hypothesis vs PSRL')}). The learned agents, which never saw a jammed lock, reach @{SRN('macro_gru',3,0.4)} (Macro-GRU) and @{SRN('macro_gruB',3,0.4)} (with beliefs) at difficulty 3, within about 0.03 of the hypothesis agent (Fig.~\ref{fig:noise}, Table~\ref{tab:p4}). At difficulty 4 every agent loses a similar amount (hypothesis @{SRN('hypothesis',4,0.0)} to @{SRN('hypothesis',4,0.4)}, PSRL @{SRN('psrl',4,0.0)} to @{SRN('psrl',4,0.4)}), so the clearest effect of noise is the damage to PSRL at difficulty 3, not a general advantage of explicit testing. Requiring a third contradiction changes the hypothesis agent by @{PS('slip 0.4, d=3: Hyp.-robust vs Hypothesis')} at difficulty 3. This is one simple kind of noise with hand-set update rules; it does not establish robustness to observation noise or partial observability.

@@TABLE_P4@@

\begin{figure}[!htbp]
