\caption{Success under jammed locks. Lines join means; bars are 95\% bootstrap intervals over units. Learned agents were trained without noise.}\label{fig:noise}
\end{figure}

\subsection{An external check: CausalGame}\label{sec:cg}
CausalGame~\cite{chen2026causalgame} is an interactive design benchmark with hidden causal mechanisms. We ran its released simulator (14 scenarios) with non-language agents on the benchmark's own budget: 10 deployments of 20 drones, then one submitted design scored on 1,000 drones against the scenario's survival threshold. Agents see only the number of drones deployed and surviving in each batch. We compare the default design, random search, hill-climbing, posterior sampling and an EV+IG agent that uses the same Bayesian linear model as posterior sampling but picks experiments by expected survival plus an information bonus (with and without the bonus); 40 sessions per agent and scenario, and the scenario is the unit of analysis. Only the survival part is evaluated: the benchmark's LLM-judged report rubric cannot be applied to these agents, and our numbers are not comparable to its leaderboard.

Every search agent beats the default design (EV+IG against default: $\Delta=@{CGD(3,'diff')!+.3f}$ [@{CGD(3,'lo')!+.3f}, @{CGD(3,'hi')!+.3f}], $p_{\mathrm{Holm}}=@{CGD(3,'p_holm')!.3f}$), but the EV+IG agent is not better than the simpler ones. Mean survival is @{CGS('Hypothesis (EV + info gain)','survival')} for EV+IG, @{CGS('Posterior sampling','survival')} for posterior sampling, @{CGS('Hill-climbing (reactive)','survival')} for hill-climbing and @{CGS('Random search','survival')} for random search; none of the differences from EV+IG is significant after correction (Table~\ref{tab:cg2}), and the information bonus makes no detectable difference ($\Delta=@{CGD(4,'diff')!+.3f}$). On the six antenna scenarios the EV+IG agent trails hill-climbing and random search by about 0.1; we did not diagnose why, and a smooth surrogate fitted to ten noisy batches may be a poor model of an optimum at the edge of the design space. Unlike the grid and the vault, no hypothesis space matching the generator is supplied here, so this result is consistent with the paper's conditional claim: the advantage of explicit hypothesis testing depends on a hypothesis space that fits the environment, and it does not transfer automatically.

@@TABLE_CG1@@

@@TABLE_CG2@@

\subsection{Can the macro vocabulary be learned?}
Generic-skill agents see only go-to-object skills and the primitives INTERACT and PUSH, so the test-the-key structure must be found. Generic-FF/GRU reach @{SR('generic_ff',3)}/@{SR('generic_gru',3)} at difficulty 3, @{SR('generic_ff',4)}/@{SR('generic_gru',4)} at 4 and @{SR('generic_ff',5)}/@{SR('generic_gru',5)} at 5, against @{SR('macro_ff',3)}/@{SR('macro_gru',3)}, @{SR('macro_ff',4)}/@{SR('macro_gru',4)} and @{SR('macro_ff',5)}/@{SR('macro_gru',5)} for Macro-FF/GRU. They were trained for about 6k episodes with only @{NGENERIC} seeds, against 12k episodes for the macro agents, and their training success had plateaued near 0.75; a longer budget may close part of the gap, which we did not test. The testing structure can be learned from generic skills, at a cost that appears to grow with difficulty; this is exploratory.

\subsection{A second family and the proposition}
In the combination vault (Fig.~\ref{fig:vault}) the explicit hypothesis agent opens the vault in @{VT('hypothesis','in_dist')!.3f} of small-code and @{VT('hypothesis','larger')!.3f} of larger-code episodes, with @{VT('hypothesis','in_dist','resets')!.2f} and @{VT('hypothesis','larger','resets')!.2f} failed presses against the elimination predictions @{VT('hypothesis','in_dist','pred_elim_resets')!.2f} and @{VT('hypothesis','larger','pred_elim_resets')!.2f} of Proposition~\ref{prop:one}. On the grid, single-gate environments give @{TH('hypothesis',3)!.3f}, @{TH('hypothesis',4)!.3f} and @{TH('hypothesis',5)!.3f} failed door tests at difficulty 3, 4 and 5, against @{TH('hypothesis',3,'pred_elim')!.3f}, @{TH('hypothesis',4,'pred_elim')!.3f} and @{TH('hypothesis',5,'pred_elim')!.3f} predicted (computed with the number of keys reachable before the door opens), while the reactive agent makes @{TH('reactive',3)!.3f}, @{TH('reactive',4)!.3f} and @{TH('reactive',5)!.3f}. Trained only on small codes, PPO-GRU reaches @{VT('vault_gru','in_dist')!.3f} success in distribution (@{VT('vault_gru','in_dist','resets')!.2f} failed presses) but @{VT('vault_gru','larger')!.3f} on larger codes (@{VT('vault_gru','larger','resets')!.1f} failed presses), and PPO-FF @{VT('vault_ff','in_dist')!.3f} and @{VT('vault_ff','larger')!.3f}, no better than the reactive agent (@{VT('reactive','in_dist')!.3f}, @{VT('reactive','larger')!.3f}) over @{NM('vault_gru')} trained models each. With no failure in 900 episodes per condition, the 95\% upper bound on the hypothesis agent's failure rate is @{RO3(900)!.2%}; the perfect score is a statement about this finite synthetic benchmark. The difference to the hypothesis agent on larger codes is @{PS('vault larger codes: Hypothesis vs PPO-GRU')} for PPO-GRU. Learned memory solved the training distribution but did not extrapolate; explicit elimination did.

\begin{figure}[!htbp]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=0.7\textwidth]{figures/fig_vault.pdf}
\caption{Combination vault. (a) Success on the training code sizes and on larger unseen sizes; dots are individual trained models. (b) Failed presses of the hypothesis agent against the elimination prediction of Proposition~\ref{prop:one}.}\label{fig:vault}
\end{figure}

\subsection{Further checks}
\textbf{Rule types.} When some doors are opened by a switch instead of a key, the hypothesis agent with a closed vocabulary scores @{RT('hyp_closed_vocab','typed_SD_d3')!.3f} and @{RT('hyp_closed_vocab','typed_SD_d4')!.3f} (difficulty 3, 4) and with an open vocabulary @{RT('hyp_open_vocab','typed_SD_d3')!.3f} and @{RT('hyp_open_vocab','typed_SD_d4')!.3f}. The reactive agent, which toggles every switch it sees, scores @{RT('reactive','typed_SD_d3')!.3f} and @{RT('reactive','typed_SD_d4')!.3f}. This is the benchmark's out-of-vocabulary test, with a rule type that is absent from the agent's templates: it is not discovered, and the simple reactive agent beats the closed-vocabulary hypothesis agent. The agent chooses among rule types it was given and does not invent new ones. \textbf{Recovery and priors.} After pre-training on identity-mapped rules, tests on mappings that violate them give @{REC('hyp_with_revision')!.3f} with revision and @{REC('hyp_no_revision')!.3f} without , while an agent without the prior scores @{REC('hyp_no_prior')!.3f}: a cross-episode prior helped when the relation persisted (regime D) and caused negative transfer when it was reversed.

\textbf{Failures.} At difficulty 5 the hypothesis agent failed @{FA['categories']['n_failed']} of @{FA['categories']['n_episodes']} episodes. A rule-based classifier (not a manual inspection) assigned them to distractor interference (@{FA['categories']['counts']['distractor interference']}), long-horizon dependency (@{FA['categories']['counts']['long-horizon dependency']}) and a repeatedly refuted hypothesis (@{FA['categories']['counts']['repeated refuted hypothesis']}). Raising the step budget from 200 to 800 lifts difficulty-5 success from @{BUD(5,200)!.2f} to @{BUD(5,800)!.2f}, so roughly half of the failures are budget-limited; the remaining @{1-BUD(5,800)!.2f} are not, and we did not inspect them episode by episode.
